**Q3: Train Round Trips Calculation**

**Given:**
*   Speed ($v$) = 60 km/h
*   Distance between cities ($d$) = 200 km
*   Time available ($T$) = 1 full day = 24 hours
*   Station time = negligible

**Steps:**
1.  **Calculate the time for a one-way trip:**
    $$\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}} = \frac{200 \text{ km}}{60 \text{ km/h}} = \frac{10}{3} \text{ hours} \approx 3.33 \text{ hours}$$
2.  **Calculate the time for a round trip:**
    A round trip consists of going from City A to City B and back to City A.
    $$\text{Round Trip Time} = 2 \times \text{One-way Time} = 2 \times \frac{10}{3} \text{ hours} = \frac{20}{3} \text{ hours} \approx 6.67 \text{ hours}$$
3.  **Calculate the number of round trips in 24 hours:**
    $$\text{Number of trips} = \frac{\text{Total Time}}{\text{Round Trip Time}} = \frac{24}{20/3} = 24 \times \frac{3}{20} = \frac{72}{20} = 3.6$$
4.  **Interpret the result:** Since the train cannot complete a partial round trip within the definition of "making a round trip," we take the whole number part.

**Answer:** The train can make **3** full round trips. (It would have time for 60% of a 4th trip, but not a full one). [end of text]


